= Discourse (linguistics) — Technical =

== Units and representations ==
Discourse theories posit different units:

* turns and turn-constructional units;
* discourse segments;
* discourse referents;
* rhetorical or coherence relations;
* propositions and Questions Under Discussion;
* adjacency pairs;
* moves/dialogue acts;
* genres and activity types.

There is no universal discourse atom.

== Coherence ==
Coherence can arise through referential continuity, causal/temporal organization, rhetorical relations, topic structure, shared schemas, pragmatic inference, and genre expectations.

Surface cohesion is evidence, not a complete definition of coherence.

== Dynamic semantics ==
Discourse representation and dynamic semantic frameworks model interpretation incrementally.

A discourse can update an information state while introducing entities whose accessibility constrains later anaphora.

== Conversation analysis ==
Conversation analysis focuses on sequential organization grounded in recorded interaction, including turn-taking, repair, preference organization, and action formation.

Its methodological commitments differ from purely formal discourse semantics.

== Computational modeling ==
Computational discourse tasks include coreference resolution, discourse parsing, coherence modeling, dialogue-state tracking, summarization, argument mining, and conversation modeling.

Long-context neural models can capture useful dependencies without making their internal states identical to linguistic discourse structures.

